You've found a hundred witchy nail posts and every single one ends the same way  with a cauldron, a broomstick, and a reminder that Halloween is coming. That's not a witchy nail. That's a costume.

<strong>Witchy nails</strong> are something else entirely. They're the matte black oval you wear in March. The deep forest green you book at the salon because something about it feels like yours. The celestial set you save to three separate boards and eventually actually get done. <strong>Witchy nails</strong> are an aesthetic  a mood, a signal, a form of self-expression that has nothing to do with what month it is. This post maps out every corner of that aesthetic so you can find the version that's been yours all along.

We're covering everything from celestial and forest witch to dark dramatic and soft minimalist  including which shapes and finishes do the most work, how short nails fit into this aesthetic (they do, fully), and where the witchy world overlaps with whimsigoth and cottagecore. For the broader dark nail landscape, <a href='/blog/goth/goth-nails'>Goth Nails: The Ultimate Guide to Dark & Edgy Nail Art</a> is the place to start.

