## Witchy Nail Shapes: Which One Matches Your Aesthetic?

Shape is not decoration. It's architecture  and for <strong>witchy nails</strong>, the shape you choose either amplifies the aesthetic or works quietly against it.

Almond is the most universally recommended shape across every witchy sub-aesthetic, and the reasoning is structural: the tapered tip and soft curve create a hand silhouette that reads as elegant and slightly otherworldly even on a bare nail. It suits celestial, forest, and soft witchy sets equally well, and the full roundup of <a href='https://mirelleinspo.com/inspo/almond-nails-2026'>Almond Nails 2026</a> explores the shape's range in detail. Coffin shapes carry more drama  the flat tip and angular sides suit dark witch and maximalist celestial sets; <a href='https://mirelleinspo.com/inspo/coffin-ballerina-nails-2026'>Coffin Nails 2026</a> is the reference point if you're drawn in this direction. Stiletto is the sharpest statement, best reserved for sets where drama is the explicit goal. Oval shapes are the most wearable and work particularly well for soft, minimal witchy designs where the shape should disappear into the overall mood rather than lead it.

Short nails: square with slightly softened corners (the "squoval") handles dark solid and matte-finish designs best. It's clean, not costume-adjacent, and looks considered at short lengths in a way that very rounded or very pointed shapes sometimes don't.

